=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: aswa1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The access VLAN assignment (PVID) on switch port aswa1.eth1 — the physical port that laptop1's desk cable terminates on.

**Mechanism:** aswa1 is an 802.1Q-aware bridge. Every port has a native (PVID) VLAN that determines which L2 broadcast domain untagged frames are placed into. aswa1.eth1 is configured with `PVID=40 Egress Untagged`, so all of laptop1's untagged traffic is stamped with VLAN tag 40 as it enters the switch fabric. VLAN 40 is routed in the 10.6.0.0/24 space with gateway 10.6.0.1 — entirely separate from the campus client VLAN 10 (10.1.0.0/24). As a result, laptop1 receives a 10.6.0.100/24 address and cannot reach pc1 on the same L2 segment even though they are one desk apart. Basic ICMP still crosses the boundary via inter-VLAN routing, but same-subnet services (broadcast-based discovery, mDNS, NetBIOS, SMB neighbourhood browsing) are broken because they do not traverse router boundaries.

**Localization:** Device **aswa1**, interface **eth1** (the port connected to collision domain CD_LINK_A2 / laptop1). The adjacent pc1 port on aswa2.eth0 is correctly set to PVID=10.

**Root cause:** aswa1.eth1 has its native VLAN misconfigured as VLAN 40 instead of the correct client VLAN 10, placing laptop1 in the wrong L2/L3 domain and stripping it of same-subnet reachability to its neighbour pc1.

**Fix:** Reconfigure aswa1.eth1's PVID to 10 (`bridge vlan add dev eth1 vid 10 pvid untagged master` and remove VLAN 40 as PVID). laptop1 will then land in VLAN 10, obtain a 10.1.0.x address, and resume direct L2 communication with pc1.
=== END ===